Here's another version of a `CompressionStream` polyfill, which seems to work enough for Drizzle Studio running with Bun.

```ts
import { Readable, Writable } from 'node:stream'
import zlib from 'node:zlib'

const transformMap = {
  'deflate': zlib.createDeflate,
  'deflate-raw': zlib.createDeflateRaw,
  'gzip': zlib.createGzip,
}

globalThis.CompressionStream ??= class CompressionStream {
  readable: ReadableStream
  writable: WritableStream

  constructor(format: keyof typeof transformMap) {
    const handle = transformMap[format]()
    this.readable = Readable.toWeb(handle)
    this.writable = Writable.toWeb(handle)
  }
}
```

*Updated snippet following comment below from @participant17*

2 things. I don't think polyfill needs to be written in typescript as there is already good typing support for it. Using pure vanilla javascript makes it cross compatible and don't require any compiling.

You should probably also polyfill both if you only need one. They kind of come hand in hand. If someone only checks for the existence of compression devs would kind of assume that decompression exist as well. 

And lastly, yes, do use ??= Instead

All in all

```js
import { Readable, Writable } from 'node:stream'
import zlib from 'node:zlib'

const transformMap = {
  'deflate': zlib.createDeflate,
  'deflate-raw': zlib.createDeflateRaw,
  'gzip': zlib.createGzip,
}

globalThis.CompressionStream ??= class CompressionStream {
  constructor(format) {
    const handle = transformMap[format]()
    this.readable = Readable.toWeb(handle)
    this.writable = Writable.toWeb(handle)
  }
}
```
